<section id="S4.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.2.1 </span><span id="S4.SS2.SSS1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ComplexEval</span>-Basic</h4>

<section id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Task &amp; Data Source</h5>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">ComplexEval-Basic extends RMB-bench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al. (2025)</a></cite>, a general preference-pair benchmark for reward modeling. Through domain-specific sampling (Appendix <a href="#A1.SS1" title="A.1 Sampling Strategy ‣ Appendix A Details of Dataset Construction ‣ Curse of Knowledge: When Complex Evaluation Context Benefits yet Biases LLM Judges" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.1</span></a>), we construct 1,056 samples <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(Q,R_{c},R_{r})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Q,R_{c},R_{r})</annotation></semantics></math> across 12 diverse scenarios (code, open-QA, etc.).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Auxiliary Information Synthesis</h5>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Since original samples lack required auxiliary information, we adopt two augmentation steps to generate reference answers and rubrics (Appendix <a href="#A1.SS2" title="A.2 Data Augmentation ‣ Appendix A Details of Dataset Construction ‣ Curse of Knowledge: When Complex Evaluation Context Benefits yet Biases LLM Judges" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.2</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Attack Process</h5>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Employing the Comprehensive Attack strategy (Section <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 Adversarial Sample Generation ‣ 4 ComplexEval ‣ Curse of Knowledge: When Complex Evaluation Context Benefits yet Biases LLM Judges" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>), we systematically perturb the weaker responses <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> in all preference pairs through both <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{ref}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{ref}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{rubric}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{rubric}</annotation></semantics></math> modifications:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{\prime}_{r}=\mathcal{A}_{GA}(R_{r},A),\quad A\in\{A_{ref},A_{rubric}\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{\prime}_{r}=\mathcal{A}_{GA}(R_{r},A),\quad A\in\{A_{ref},A_{rubric}\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.2.2 </span>ComplexEval-Advanced</h4>

<section id="S4.SS2.SSS2.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Tasks &amp; Data Source</h5>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS2.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS2.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">ComplexEval-Advanced focuses on three challenging domains requiring sophisticated evaluation. Through Random sampling (Appendix <a href="#A1.SS1" title="A.1 Sampling Strategy ‣ Appendix A Details of Dataset Construction ‣ Curse of Knowledge: When Complex Evaluation Context Benefits yet Biases LLM Judges" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.1</span></a>), we curate 400 high-complexity single-response samples <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(Q,R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Q,R)</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS2.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S4.I1" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Mathematical Reasoning</span>: mathematical reasoning from ProcessBench’s Olympiad competition data<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, with Official Olympiad solutions as <math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{ref}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{ref}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Creative Writing</span>: creative writing from WritingBench<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2025b</a>)</cite> with multi-dimensional scoring systems as <math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{rubric}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{rubric}</annotation></semantics></math> and gold stories as <math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{ref}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{ref}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Role-Playing</span>: The most challenging scenario from the <span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">CoSER</span> dataset<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025b</a>)</cite>, featuring canonical dialogues <math id="S4.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{ref}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{ref}</annotation></semantics></math> augmented with comprehensive character backgrounds and otherauxiliary information, evaluated via professional dramatic consistency rubrics <math id="S4.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{rubric}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{rubric}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS2.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Attack Process</h5>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We apply Target Attack (Section <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 Adversarial Sample Generation ‣ 4 ComplexEval ‣ Curse of Knowledge: When Complex Evaluation Context Benefits yet Biases LLM Judges" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>) to systematically probe six bias mechanisms. For each sample <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(Q_{i},R_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Q_{i},R_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, perturbations are generated through type-consistent transformations:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{\prime}_{i}=\mathcal{A}_{TA}(R_{i},\phi_{k}),\quad\phi_{k}=R_{k}(A_{i})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{\prime}_{i}=\mathcal{A}_{TA}(R_{i},\phi_{k}),\quad\phi_{k}=R_{k}(A_{i})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Metric</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To ensure consistency across ComplexEval’s tiered structure (Basic: preference pairs; Advanced: single responses), we implement a unified point-wise scoring approach based on recent GRM methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For any query <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> with auxiliary information <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>, the evaluation process computes:<math id="S4.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S=M(Q,R,A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=M(Q,R,A)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the judge model’s scoring function.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">For ComplexEval-Basic</h5>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We quantify judgment correctness using pairwise comparisons. While point-wise evaluation is operationally convenient, it requires special handling of score ties—overly strict equality criteria would inflate error rates. We therefore introduce a tolerance threshold <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta=0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta=0.5</annotation></semantics></math> to define ambiguous judgments when scores are functionally equivalent(Appendix <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Point-wise judge ‣ Curse of Knowledge: When Complex Evaluation Context Benefits yet Biases LLM Judges" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>):</p>
<table id="S4.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J=\begin{cases}1,&amp;S_{c}^{ref}&gt;S_{r}^{ref}+\theta\\
0.5,&amp;|S_{c}^{ref}-S_{r}^{ref}|\leq\theta\\
0,&amp;S_{c}^{ref}&lt;S_{r}^{ref}-\theta\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>0.5</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>−</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J=\begin{cases}1,&amp;S_{c}^{ref}&gt;S_{r}^{ref}+\theta\\
0.5,&amp;|S_{c}^{ref}-S_{r}^{ref}|\leq\theta\\
0,&amp;S_{c}^{ref}&lt;S_{r}^{ref}-\theta\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">This <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> value was empirically determined to balance sensitivity and specificity across our 12 scenarios. We then define two complementary metrics:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><span id="S4.Ex4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">ACC</span></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{1}{|D|}\sum_{i=1}^{|D|}\mathbb{I}(\hat{J}_{i}=J_{i}^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mi>𝕀</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>J</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{1}{|D|}\sum_{i=1}^{|D|}\mathbb{I}(\hat{J}_{i}=J_{i}^{*})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><span id="S4.Ex5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">RR</span></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{1}{|D|}\sum_{i=1}^{|D|}\mathbb{I}(\hat{J}_{i}=J_{i})\quad" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mi>𝕀</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{1}{|D|}\sum_{i=1}^{|D|}\mathbb{I}(\hat{J}_{i}=J_{i})\quad</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>J</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes ground-truth preferences and <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{J}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{J}</annotation></semantics></math> represents judgments under bias-inducing conditions, <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|D|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|D|</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the dataset size.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">For ComplexEval-Advanced</h5>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">, the absence of preference labels prevents direct accuracy measurement. Instead, we employ attack success rate (ASR) as our primary bias metric, quantifying how frequently adversarial perturbations (<math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\rightarrow R^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\rightarrow R^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>) induce significant score changes. This approach aligns with security evaluation paradigms where:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S4.I2" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S4.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Mathematical Reasoning</span>: An attack succeeds when the model correctly locates errors originally (<math id="S4.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Loc}_{\text{orig}}=\text{Loc}_{\text{gt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext>Loc</mtext><mtext>orig</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mtext>Loc</mtext><mtext>gt</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Loc}_{\text{orig}}=\text{Loc}_{\text{gt}}</annotation></semantics></math>) but fails after perturbation (<math id="S4.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Loc}_{\text{adv}}=\neq\text{Loc}_{\text{gt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext>Loc</mtext><mtext>adv</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≠</mo><msub><mtext>Loc</mtext><mtext>gt</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Loc}_{\text{adv}}=\neq\text{Loc}_{\text{gt}}</annotation></semantics></math>):</p>
<table id="S4.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Win}=\mathbb{I}(\text{Loc}_{\text{orig}}=\text{Loc}_{\text{gt}}\land\text{Loc}_{\text{adv}}\neq\text{Loc}_{\text{gt}})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Win</mtext><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝕀</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mtext>Loc</mtext><mtext>orig</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mtext>Loc</mtext><mtext>gt</mtext></msub><mo>∧</mo><msub><mtext>Loc</mtext><mtext>adv</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>≠</mo><msub><mtext>Loc</mtext><mtext>gt</mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Win}=\mathbb{I}(\text{Loc}_{\text{orig}}=\text{Loc}_{\text{gt}}\land\text{Loc}_{\text{adv}}\neq\text{Loc}_{\text{gt}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Creative Writing/Role-Playing</span>: Success requires exceeding domain-specific improvement thresholds (<math id="S4.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{\text{role}}=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mtext>role</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{\text{role}}=10</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{\text{writing}}=0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mtext>writing</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{\text{writing}}=0.5</annotation></semantics></math>), calibrated to each rubric’s score distribution:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Win}=\mathbb{I}(S_{\text{adv}}-S_{\text{orig}}\geq\beta)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Win</mtext><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝕀</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mtext>adv</mtext></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mtext>orig</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Win}=\mathbb{I}(S_{\text{adv}}-S_{\text{orig}}\geq\beta)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The overall ASR then provides a standardized bias measure:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{ASR}=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\text{Win}_{i}\quad" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>ASR</mtext><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><msub><mtext>Win</mtext><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{ASR}=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\text{Win}_{i}\quad</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> represents attackable samples number.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T3" class="ltx_table">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure ltx_flex_table">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S4.T3.fig1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_top" style="width:165.6pt;">
<table id="S4.T3.fig1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S4.T3.fig1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T3.fig1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:7.0pt;padding-right:7.0pt;" rowspan="2"><span id="S4.T3.fig1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text">Model</span></th>
<th id="S4.T3.fig1.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:7.0pt;padding-right:7.0pt;" colspan="3"><span id="S4.T3.fig1.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">original samples</span></th></tr>
<tr id="S4.T3.fig1.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T3.fig1.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:7.0pt;padding-right:7.0pt;"><math id="S4.T3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Acc}_{\text{orig.}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>Acc</mtext><mtext>orig.</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Acc}_{\text{orig.}}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="S4.T3.fig1.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:7.0pt;padding-right:7.0pt;"><math id="S4.T3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Acc}_{\text{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>Acc</mtext><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Acc}_{\text{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="S4.T3.fig1.1.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:7.0pt;padding-right:7.0pt;"><math id="S4.T3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Acc}_{\text{rubrics}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>Acc</mtext><mtext>rubrics</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Acc}_{\text{rubrics}}</annotation></semantics></math></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S4.T3.fig1.1.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T3.fig1.1.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:7.0pt;padding-right:7.0pt;"><span id="S4.T3.fig1.1.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">General model</span></th>
<td id="S4.T3.fig1.1.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:7.0pt;padding-right:7.0pt;"></td>
<td id="S4.T3.fig1.1.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:7.0pt;padding-right:7.0pt;"></td>
<td id="S4.T3.fig1.1.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:7.0pt;padding-right:7.0pt;"></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T3.fig1.1.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T3.fig1.1.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_r" style="padding-left:7.0pt;padding-right:7.0pt;">qwen2.5-32b</th>
<td id="S4.T3.fig1.1.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-left:7.0pt;padding-right:7.0pt;">67.14</td>
